<h2>A major staple market that needs a different operating playbook</h2>
<p>Basmati is, by a wide margin, the largest Indian export line into Yemen — over USD 360 million in 2024, putting it among India's largest single-country basmati markets despite a genuinely difficult logistics environment. That combination — huge staple demand, complicated routing — is exactly why we handle this lane differently from our other basmati markets rather than applying the same playbook we'd use for the UAE or Saudi Arabia.</p>

<h2>Packaging built for handling variability, not just transit time</h2>
<p>On a predictable short Gulf lane, standard export bag specifications are generally sufficient. For Yemen, we favour heavier-gauge PP bags with reinforced stitching, because the handling conditions between vessel discharge and final inland delivery are less consistent than on our more established routes. This is a genuine specification difference, not a cosmetic one — it's the single easiest thing to get wrong if a Yemen order is packed to the same spec as a routine Saudi shipment.</p>

<h2>Documentation prepared for a routing change, not just a routing plan</h2>
<p>Because port availability and routing between Aden and Hodeidah can shift at short notice given current conditions, we prepare shipping documentation so it holds up even if the destination port changes after the vessel has sailed — rather than documentation tied tightly to a single assumed port of discharge. That's a deliberate sequencing choice specific to this lane, built from experience with how Yemen shipments actually move, not a generic caution.</p>

<h2>Grade expectations are standard — the operational plan around them isn't</h2>
<p>It's worth being clear that Yemeni buyers want the same core product as the rest of the Middle East — long-grain, meaningfully aged 1121 basmati. Nothing about the grade specification changes here. What changes is everything around it: packaging robustness, documentation sequencing, and payment structuring agreed case by case (see our Yemen market page for the payment detail). Treat this as an operations conversation as much as a product one.</p>

